Colleagues,

This is the summary of Saturday's TapLine outage that I promised on the
weekend call. A full report will follow within ten working days.

What happened

At 06:12 on Saturday 14 February, TapLine validators began rejecting every
card presented. The fault affected 412 validators: all of those on Routes
14 and 22, on route C1, and at the gates for route F2. Validators on other
routes were not affected because they connect through the older payment
gateway.

Service was not interrupted. Following the standing instruction for
validator failure, drivers and gate staff let passengers travel without
tapping. The fault was cleared at 10:47, so the outage lasted 4 hours and
35 minutes. We classed it as a Severity 1 incident.

Cause

The security certificate on the newer payment gateway, TL-GW2, expired at
06:00 that morning. The certificate had been installed by our supplier,
Syntrel Systems, when the gateway went live two years ago. Its expiry date
was not recorded in our own monitoring, and Syntrel's reminder went to the
mailbox of an engineer who left them last year.

The on-call engineer was paged at 06:20 and identified the cause at 08:05.
Most of the remaining time was spent waiting for Syntrel to issue and
install a new certificate, because we do not hold the rights to do that
ourselves.

Impact

- Fares not collected: we estimate $38,600, based on the same Saturday in
  the previous three weeks.
- Passengers: roughly 21,000 boardings were made without a tap. Nobody was
  charged incorrectly and no penalty fares were issued on the affected
  routes during the outage.
- Personal data: none was exposed or lost. I have confirmed this with the
  Data Protection Officer, and no report to the regulator is needed.
- Customer Centre: 164 calls about the fault between 06:30 and 11:00.

Supplier position

Our contract with Syntrel requires 99.9% availability in each calendar
month. Saturday's outage alone takes February below that. The remedy in the
contract is a service credit of 5% of the monthly fee. The monthly fee is
$64,000, so the credit is small compared with the lost fares. I have asked
Cormac's team whether we can recover the lost fare revenue as well and will
report back.

Actions

1. All certificates on both gateways have been checked. The next expiry is
   on the older gateway on 9 August 2026.
2. Certificate expiry dates are now in our monitoring system, with alerts at
   30 days, 14 days and 3 days before expiry. Done on Sunday.
3. Syntrel will give our on-call engineers the rights to install a
   certificate themselves. Due by 27 February.
4. We will test failover from TL-GW2 to the older gateway, which should have
   taken over automatically and did not. Due by 31 March.
5. I will bring the full report, including the failover finding, to the
   Audit and Risk Committee at its next meeting after tomorrow's.

One point for the validator upgrade in next year's capital programme: the
new validators will be able to accept taps offline and settle them later,
which would have made Saturday a non-event for passengers and for revenue.

Please do not circulate the lost-fares figure outside this group. Sunniva
has a short public line if anyone is asked: there was a technical fault with
card readers on some routes on Saturday morning, nobody was overcharged, and
it has been fixed.

